# platforms/proxmox/config.sh-template
#
# Copy to config.sh (git-ignored — never commit this file, it names your own
# machines) and fill in your own values. Sourced by every script in
# platforms/proxmox/scripts/.
#
# Before filling this in: each of the 3 hosts below needs Proxmox already
# installed, a ZFS pool/storage already created, and the three joined into
# one Proxmox cluster. That part is NOT scripted — see README.md's
# "Before you start" section for why (one step genuinely needs a human
# typing a password) and the exact commands to run it yourself.

# ── The three Proxmox hosts ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# host3 is assumed the weakest machine of the three in the defaults below
# (it gets a lighter VM and a control-plane-only k3s role) — reorder which
# physical machine is host1/2/3 to match your own hardware, not the names.
PROXMOX_HOST1_NAME="host1"
PROXMOX_HOST1_ADDR="192.168.1.10"
PROXMOX_HOST2_NAME="host2"
PROXMOX_HOST2_ADDR="192.168.1.11"
PROXMOX_HOST3_NAME="host3"
PROXMOX_HOST3_ADDR="192.168.1.12"

# 🔴 THE LAN EVERY K3S NODE AND EVERY CORE-SERVICES GUEST SHARES — not optional metadata.
# Found running this for real (2026-10-05): a k3s pod talking to an external-service proxy (e.g.
# `uis deploy postgresql` pointed at "pg" or "pg-test") has its traffic SNAT'd to the k3s NODE's
# own address before it reaches the guest — postgres then sees a connection from THAT address,
# not from inside the cluster, and refuses it outright with "no pg_hba.conf entry for host
# ...". Same CIDR as your three Proxmox hosts' own addresses above, just wide enough to also
# cover every k3s VM and core-services guest DHCP hands out. If you widened/narrowed the subnet
# from the default /22, update this to match.
CORE_SERVICES_LAN_CIDR="192.168.1.0/22"

# The SSH key ansible uses to reach every Proxmox host AND every k3s VM this
# creates. scripts/00-storage-ensure.sh generates it on first run and installs
# it on all 3 hosts; every later script just assumes it already works.
PROXMOX_SSH_KEY="${HOME}/.ssh/id_ed25519_proxmox"

# Proxmox cluster name (what `pvecm create <name>` was run with) and the ZFS
# storage name every host registered (see README) — must be identical on all
# three hosts, that's what makes a guest movable between them.
PROXMOX_CLUSTER_NAME="homelab"
PROXMOX_STORAGE="tank-vm"

# ── Networking ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The k3s VMs need 6 free static addresses on the SAME subnet as the 3
# Proxmox hosts above (2 per host: production + mirror test cluster). Must
# not already be in use or handed out by your DHCP server.
NETWORK_PREFIX_LENGTH="24"          # match your LAN's netmask (e.g. 22, 24)
NETWORK_GATEWAY="192.168.1.1"
NETWORK_DNS="192.168.1.1"
NETWORK_SEARCH_DOMAIN="lan"

K3S_VM_HOST1_IP="192.168.1.30"       # production, --cluster-init
K3S_VM_HOST1_TEST_IP="192.168.1.31"  # mirror test cluster, --cluster-init
K3S_VM_HOST2_IP="192.168.1.32"       # production, joins host1
K3S_VM_HOST2_TEST_IP="192.168.1.33"  # test, joins host1-test
K3S_VM_HOST3_IP="192.168.1.34"       # production, control-plane-only — see README
K3S_VM_HOST3_TEST_IP="192.168.1.35"  # test, joins host1-test

# ── VM sizing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Defaults are what the reference lab used — see README's "Sizing" section
# for the reasoning (host2 had the most headroom, host3 the least). Tune for
# your own hardware; these are the only numbers that matter for that.
K3S_VM_HOST1_CORES=2;      K3S_VM_HOST1_MEMORY_MB=4096;      K3S_VM_HOST1_DISK_GB=20
K3S_VM_HOST1_TEST_CORES=1; K3S_VM_HOST1_TEST_MEMORY_MB=2048; K3S_VM_HOST1_TEST_DISK_GB=10
K3S_VM_HOST2_CORES=4;      K3S_VM_HOST2_MEMORY_MB=6144;      K3S_VM_HOST2_DISK_GB=20
K3S_VM_HOST2_TEST_CORES=2; K3S_VM_HOST2_TEST_MEMORY_MB=2048; K3S_VM_HOST2_TEST_DISK_GB=10
K3S_VM_HOST3_CORES=2;      K3S_VM_HOST3_MEMORY_MB=3072;      K3S_VM_HOST3_DISK_GB=10
K3S_VM_HOST3_TEST_CORES=1; K3S_VM_HOST3_TEST_MEMORY_MB=2048; K3S_VM_HOST3_TEST_DISK_GB=10

# host3's PRODUCTION k3s node contributes its HA vote and nothing else — no
# workload pods are scheduled there. Set to false if host3 isn't your
# weakest/slowest-disk machine. See README for why this exists.
K3S_VM_HOST3_CONTROL_PLANE_ONLY=true

# VMIDs — must be free on each respective Proxmox host.
K3S_VM_HOST1_VMID=490;  K3S_VM_HOST1_TEST_VMID=491
K3S_VM_HOST2_VMID=290;  K3S_VM_HOST2_TEST_VMID=291
K3S_VM_HOST3_VMID=190;  K3S_VM_HOST3_TEST_VMID=191

# ── VM identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
VM_CLOUD_IMAGE="noble-server-cloudimg-amd64.img"
VM_CLOUD_IMAGE_URL="https://cloud-images.ubuntu.com/noble/current/noble-server-cloudimg-amd64.img"
VM_CIUSER="k3s"

# ── k3s ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Pin an EXACT version, not a floating channel — see README for why. Check
# the current one at https://update.k3s.io/v1-release/channels (the "stable"
# channel's value) rather than trusting a value in this template to still be
# current.
K3S_VERSION="v1.36.5+k3s1"

# Deliberately different from production's k3s defaults (10.42.0.0/16 /
# 10.43.0.0/16) so `ip route`/`kubectl` output is never ambiguous about which
# cluster you're looking at.
K3S_TEST_CLUSTER_CIDR="10.52.0.0/16"
K3S_TEST_SERVICE_CIDR="10.53.0.0/16"

# ── Core services — LXC, not k3s pods ──────────────────────────────────────
# pg (database), bao (secrets), garage (object storage), registry (image
# cache), forgejo (git host + its own built-in OCI registry), pg-test (a
# SEPARATE, smaller postgres instance — not a database inside "pg" — for the
# test k3s cluster, isolated from production's; see scripts/point-postgresql-
# at.sh for switching which one the active cluster target talks to). Remove
# any you don't want; order doesn't matter. Add one at a time later with
# `uis platform core-service add proxmox <name>` instead of hand-editing this
# line and re-running the whole apply chain.
# All build on host1 by default — not a hardware-strength ranking like k3s's
# host3 rule (see README): every node is an equal HA failover target
# regardless of strength, so concentrating them is just the simplest layout,
# not a safety requirement.
#
# 🔵 THIS DEFAULT IS THE REFERENCE LAB'S OWN REAL CONFIGURATION (Terje,
# 2026-10-21): a fresh lab owner running the init wizard gets our proven
# settings, not generic placeholders. Reconciled field-by-field against the
# real lab's live state before this default was set, including the two
# additions here (forgejo, pg-test) — not guessed.
CORE_SERVICES="pg bao garage registry forgejo pg-test"

# 🔴 REQUIRED FOR EVERY SERVICE LISTED ABOVE — NOT AN OPTIONAL OVERRIDE like the blocks below.
# Live-confirmed, urb-agents#1946: bao was the first genuinely FRESH CORE_SERVICES guest this
# convention ever actually created (every other service found a pre-existing guest and skipped
# the create path entirely) — and it came up on a plain DHCP lease, because nothing upstream of
# `pct create` ever had an IP to put in it. A DHCP lease is not a declared address: it can change
# on a relocation or lease renewal, silently breaking anything that expects this guest to stay put
# (its own nftables allow-list entries elsewhere, DNS, bao's cluster auth wiring). Same LAN/CIDR as
# the Proxmox hosts and k3s VMs above — pick 6 free addresses outside the k3s VM range (.30-.35 by
# default); the values below are placeholders, not a real lab's addresses. Only the services you
# actually listed in CORE_SERVICES need a real value here; an unused one is never read, and
# confirm/repair on an already-existing guest never touches its live network config either way —
# this only matters the moment a guest is actually freshly created.
CORE_SERVICE_PG_IP="192.168.1.20"
CORE_SERVICE_BAO_IP="192.168.1.21"
CORE_SERVICE_GARAGE_IP="192.168.1.22"
CORE_SERVICE_REGISTRY_IP="192.168.1.23"
CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_IP="192.168.1.24"
CORE_SERVICE_PG_TEST_IP="192.168.1.25"

# 🔴 ONLY IF ADOPTING AN EXISTING GUEST BUILT BEFORE THIS CONVENTION EXISTED. Each service's VMID
# normally comes from the fixed defaults below (405/406/407/408/409/410) — override ONE here only
# when your real hardware already has a different, unrelated guest at that VMID, or your real
# service guest was built independently at a different one. Live-confirmed, urb-agents#1940: a
# lab adopting an existing Forgejo built via an earlier ad-hoc process (not this convention) had
# it at VMID 411, not the convention's 410 — 410 on that same cluster belonged to a completely
# unrelated, unrelated guest (dns-resolver). `core-service add proxmox forgejo`'s own scoped-run
# IP lookup silently resolved THAT guest's address instead and wrote it into production's
# registries.yaml. Set the ONE that's wrong for your lab; leave the rest commented out.
# CORE_SERVICE_PG_VMID=405
# CORE_SERVICE_PG_TEST_VMID=406
# CORE_SERVICE_GARAGE_VMID=407
# CORE_SERVICE_BAO_VMID=408
# CORE_SERVICE_REGISTRY_VMID=409
# CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_VMID=410

# 🔴 EACH SERVICE'S NFTABLES FIREWALL ALLOW-LIST — empty by design in the role itself ("declare
# the real allow-list per site, not in a reusable role"), and nothing upstream of the role was
# ever wired to supply one. Live-confirmed, urb-agents#1944: a confirm/repair run on an
# already-firewalled guest SILENTLY REPLACED the real, live allow-list with this empty default —
# caught and reverted by hand on garage before it mattered; would not have been low-stakes on
# forgejo (locks out its own CI pipeline) or bao (locks out admin access). Comma-separated
# nftables range syntax ("start-end"; a single host is its own address on both ends — e.g.
# "192.168.1.50-192.168.1.50"). Leave unset for a service with no real consumers yet — an
# explicit empty allow-list is fine; this is only unsafe when it happens BY ACCIDENT.
# 🔵 registry has no entry here: its access control is HTTP-level (anonymousPolicy), not
# nftables — see roles/registry. pg and pg-test SHARE one value (CORE_SERVICE_PG_*): both run
# through the same generated/vars/core-services/_extra-vars.yml, so they cannot currently have
# DIFFERENT real-world allow-lists — only that they have the SAME one, or neither does.
# CORE_SERVICE_GARAGE_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""
# CORE_SERVICE_BAO_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""
# CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""
# CORE_SERVICE_PG_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""

# 🔴 ONLY IF ADOPTING AN EXISTING FORGEJO — its own app secrets have no auto-generate-and-persist
# mechanism at all (unlike garage's bootstrap credential, which this tool invents and keeps for
# you). Forgejo's role asserts these are supplied and never defaults them — correctly, since a
# missing JWT_SECRET makes Forgejo self-write one into its own read-only app.ini and crash-loop.
# For a BRAND-NEW forgejo with no prior secrets, generate all three once (needs docker and the
# pinned image — see roles/forgejo/defaults/main.yml for forgejo_image — on any host, not
# necessarily this one) and set them here so they survive every future run:
#   docker run --rm <forgejo_image> forgejo generate secret SECRET_KEY
#   docker run --rm <forgejo_image> forgejo generate secret INTERNAL_TOKEN
#   docker run --rm <forgejo_image> forgejo generate secret JWT_SECRET
# For an ADOPTED, already-running forgejo (urb-agents#1945: a live instance with real admin
# accounts and app-level OAuth references this tool must not rotate), set these to the guest's
# OWN existing values instead of generating fresh ones — Gitea/Forgejo invalidates every active
# session and issued token when these change, so replacing them on a running instance is a
# rotation with real consequences, not a safe default to fall back on.
# CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_SECRET_KEY=""
# CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_INTERNAL_TOKEN=""
# CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_JWT_SECRET=""

# ── Lab extras — opt-in, NOT part of CORE_SERVICES ──────────────────────────
# 🔵 EMPTY BY DEFAULT, deliberately, unlike CORE_SERVICES above. These are guests judged genuinely
# useful to SOME labs but not assumed as a UIS default — see README.md's "Lab extras" section and
# urb-agents#1970/#1971/#1972 for the reasoning (the same "generic IaC vs lab-specific" test that
# got the `nas` role removed from this platform entirely still applies; Terje's explicit call for
# dns-resolver specifically was to build it in anyway, opt-in). Add one at a time with
# `uis platform lab-extra add proxmox <name>` — never by hand-editing this line and re-running the
# whole apply chain, same convention as CORE_SERVICES/core-service add.
LAB_EXTRAS=""

# dns-resolver — dnsmasq-based LAN resolver. Generates a hosts-zone from every guest THIS platform
# itself declares (3 Proxmox hosts, 6 k3s VMs, every configured CORE_SERVICES/lab-extras guest) —
# not from a wider inventory this platform doesn't have. Tier-0: `startup: order=0` in its guest
# declaration makes Proxmox itself boot it before everything else, including registry's order=1 —
# no extra orchestration needed, the same native mechanism every CORE_SERVICES guest's own
# `startup=` already uses.
# 🔴 NOTHING IS REPOINTED AT IT AUTOMATICALLY. Every k3s VM and CORE_SERVICES guest keeps using
# NETWORK_DNS (your router) above unless you deliberately change it — that's a separate, later,
# one-consumer-at-a-time decision, not something enabling this guest does for you.
# 🔴 VMID DEFAULT IS 400, NOT 410 — the reference lab's own real dns-resolver guest happens to run
# at 410, but THIS template's own CORE_SERVICE_FORGEJO_VMID already defaults to 410 too (see
# urb-agents#1940's override note above) — defaulting both to the same number here would collide
# for any fresh lab owner who enables both. Override via LAB_EXTRA_DNS_RESOLVER_VMID if 400 is
# already taken, or if you're adopting a real dns-resolver guest already at a different VMID
# (410, matching the reference lab, is the common case for that).
LAB_EXTRA_DNS_RESOLVER_VMID="400"
# Only needs a real value once you've actually added "dns-resolver" to LAB_EXTRAS above — same
# "unused is never read" rule as the CORE_SERVICE_*_IP block.
LAB_EXTRA_DNS_RESOLVER_IP="192.168.1.26"

# 🔴 THE ONE DELIBERATE EXCEPTION TO "NEVER DEFAULT A REAL ALLOW-LIST" (urb-agents#1944's own
# rule, followed by every CORE_SERVICE_*_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES above): this guest's whole purpose
# is "every machine on the LAN queries it", so generate-ansible-config.sh fills this from
# CORE_SERVICES_LAN_CIDR above — the SAME generic "the whole LAN" value pg/pg-test's own
# pg_allowed_networks already reuses — when left unset. Override here for a narrower range,
# including explicitly empty ("") to go stricter than the default.
# LAB_EXTRA_DNS_RESOLVER_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""

# nas — Samba file server. Shares are Proxmox-managed storage volumes (mp0/mp1/...), deliberately
# NOT host bind mounts — `pvesr` cannot replicate a bind mount, so its data would not follow this
# guest on HA failover; as Proxmox volumes, the service and its data move together (see
# roles/nas/tasks/main.yml's own header). urb-agents#1970/#1978: same "generic IaC vs
# lab-specific" test as dns-resolver, same answer (Terje's explicit call, having seen the
# `LAB_EXTRAS` mechanism dns-resolver already proved out).
# 🔴 VMID DEFAULT IS 404, matching the reference lab's own real nas guest — unlike dns-resolver's
# 400-not-410, nothing else in this template already claims 404, so no collision to avoid here.
LAB_EXTRA_NAS_VMID="404"
LAB_EXTRA_NAS_IP="192.168.1.27"

# "path:size_gb" pairs, comma-separated — one Proxmox-managed storage volume per declared share.
# Each path here must match one `nas_shares[].path` entry in your own site-data file (see
# README.md's "Lab extras" section and platforms/proxmox/lab-extras-site-data/nas-site-data.yml-template).
# 🔴 ONE SHARE BY DEFAULT, not the reference lab's two — its second share is deliberately
# guest-writable with NO AUTHENTICATION at all (a real, informed, site-specific trust decision:
# Terje, 2026-10-01, "the LAN is trusted"), not something a generic UIS default should hand a
# fresh lab owner. Add a second "path:size_gb" pair here (and a matching nas_shares entry in your
# site-data file) if you want one.
LAB_EXTRA_NAS_SHARE_MOUNTS="/srv/shared:50"

# Same whole-LAN-by-default exception as LAB_EXTRA_DNS_RESOLVER_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES above, and
# for the same reason: nas serves humans/agents directly over the whole LAN, not just other
# guests. Override here for a narrower range, including explicitly empty ("") to go stricter.
# LAB_EXTRA_NAS_FIREWALL_ALLOW_RANGES=""
